    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **259 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization applied whenever an owner is created, all within `OwnerRestControllerV1` (the only endpoint that creates owners \u2014 V2 only lists them).\n\n**Changes to `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:**\n\n1. **New `normalizeAddress(String)` helper** \u2014 trims and collapses internal whitespace, upper-cases, and expands common street-type abbreviations token-by-token via an `ADDRESS_ABBREVIATIONS` map (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). An abbreviation is matched as a whole token, tolerating a single trailing full stop, so both `St` and `St.` expand to `STREET` \u2014 which matters because the app's own address format uses trailing periods (e.g. `110 W. Liberty St.`). Non-abbreviation tokens are preserved verbatim. Returns `\"\"` for null/blank input.\n\n2. **In `addOwner`** \u2014 right after mapping the DTO to an `Owner`, the address is normalized:\n   - If it is blank after normalization, the request is rejected with `400 Bad Request` (the required-field check).\n   - Otherwise the normalized value is stored on the owner via `setAddress`, so it is what gets persisted and returned in the response.\n   - Because the normalized address is set before the household block runs, both household duplicate detection and the shared `householdId` derivation now operate on the normalized form (they read `owner.getAddress()`, and existing owners were themselves stored normalized when created).\n\n3. Added the `java.util.Map` import.\n\nThe existing whitespace-only case (`\"   \"`) is still caught by the DTO's bean-validation pattern and reported in the `errors` array; the new controller-level check enforces the same rule for the normalized value as a consistent safety net per the spec.",
